            "target_entity_instance": "elevation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "These datasets were retrieved from WorldClim which was based on Shuttle Radar Topography Mission (SRTM) used to model baseline habitat suitability for both N. tabacum and Meloidogyne spp. For future suitability modelling, downscaled climate projections were used from CMIP6 (Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6), covering 23 global climate models (GCMs) across three Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs): SSP1-2.6 (SSP126: low emissions), SSP2-4.5 (SSP245: moderate emissions) and SSP5-8.5 (SSP585: high emissions) developed under the IPCC AR6 framework (IPCC, 2021). Soil data were obtained from the International Soil Reference and Information Centre (ISRIC Soil Grids) at a spatial resolution of 250 m (Hengl et al., 2017), while elevation data were sourced from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA). Soil covariates included soil pH in H2O, soil organic carbon (g/dm\u00b3), and soil texture class based on the USDA classification system. Global digital elevation model (DEM) were derived from the ETOPO1 Global Relief Model, developed by the National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI), NOAA. The ETOPO1 dataset is a cell-registered global digital elevation model with a spatial resolution of 1 arc-minute (~1.8 km), integrating terrestrial topography and ocean bathymetry, with elevation values referenced to mean sea level. The processed elevation layer was subsequently included alongside climatic and edaphic variables in the species distribution modelling framework for both baseline and soil edaphic scenarios. The predictions were drawn based on the bioclimatic variables as well as these three soil parameters with elevation taken into account simultaneously.",
